<task id="Email_T2_HowToVerifyEmailAuthentication_SD_">
  <title>How to Verify Email Authentication</title>

  <shortdesc>Learn how to check whether a Gmail message is authenticated and
  recognize indicators of an unauthenticated sender.</shortdesc>

  <taskbody>
    <context>When an email isn't authenticated, that means Gmail doesn't know
    if the message is coming from the person who appears to be sending it. If
    you see a question mark next to the sender's name, the message isn't
    authenticated. If you see this, be careful about replying or downloading
    any attachments. <note type="important"><p>Messages that aren't
    authenticated aren't necessarily spam. Sometimes authentication doesn't
    work for real organizations who send mail to big groups, like messages
    sent to mailing lists. </p></note></context>

    <steps>
      <step>
        <cmd>Open Gmail on your computer or Android device.</cmd>
      </step>

      <step>
        <cmd>Open the email you want to verify.</cmd>
      </step>

      <step>
        <cmd>Below the sender's name, click the Down arrow.</cmd>

        <info><image href="images/gmail-down-arrow.png.png"/></info>
      </step>

      <step>
        <cmd>View the email's authentication details.</cmd>

        <info>The message is authenticated if you see:<ul>
            <li>"Mailed-by" header with the domain name, like google.com.
            </li>

            <li>"Signed-by" header with the sending domain. </li>
          </ul>The message isn't authenticated if you see a question mark next
        to the sender's name. If you see this, be careful about replying or
        downloading any attachments. </info>
      </step>
    </steps>
  </taskbody>
</task>
